					"description": "Create or update an OAuth integration connection for the signed-in user. Names are normalized to a canonical lowercase-kebab provider key. Partial updates merge into the existing connection; matching client credentials reuse a shared OAuth app across connections. Do not persist access or refresh tokens here or with secretSet — `/connect/oauth` and `integrationTokenRefresh` write those on the connection. authorization.scopes is reconnect metadata: the list the next /connect/oauth visit requests, not the current access token. After widening scopes, tell the user the token is unchanged until they reconnect, then ask whether to reconnect each affected account at /connect/oauth?provider=<connection-name>. Scopes are per connection; sibling accounts that share an OAuth app keep their own lists. This capability cannot change usageMode or add requiredHosts / retarget tokenUrl to an unapproved host. Platform (built-in) connections accept only authorization.scopes here (pass authorization with the authorizeUrl from integrationGet and the full new scope list, adding or removing scopes). Every scope must be on the built-in app's supported list, and the list cannot be empty. This changes only that one connection: the built-in defaults and sibling connections keep their own scopes, and the connection stays built-in. The current token keeps its old grant until the user reconnects at /connect/oauth?provider=<name>, where the provider asks for consent. After reconnecting, integrationGet returns grantedScopes (what the provider actually granted, with missing and extra versus the request); report any mismatch instead of claiming the scopes changed.",
